\title{Binomial coefficient properties}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Here is a table of the first few [binomial coefficients](00GH), with #{n} along the left side, and #{k} along the top.}

\p{##{\begin{array}{c|cccccc}
    & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\ \hline
  0 & 1 &   &   &   &   &   \\
  1 & 1 & 1 &   &   &   &   \\
  2 & 1 & 2 & 1 &   &   &   \\
  3 & 1 & 3 & 3 & 1 &   &   \\
  4 & 1 & 4 & 6 & 4 & 1 &   \\
  5 & 1 & 5 & 10& 10& 5 & 1 \\
\end{array}}}

\p{(The blank entries are #{0}, since they represent clearly impossible things like “the number of ways to pick #{4} things out of a set of size #{2}”.) This is called \em{Pascal's triangle} and is rather well-known (you may very well have seen it before). Some properties of binomial coefficients:

\ul{
  \li{#{\displaystyle \binom{n}{0} = \binom{n}{n} = 1}. We can prove this either by looking at the formula (#{\frac{n!}{0!n!} = 1}) or by thinking about what it means combinatorially: there is exactly one way to choose nothing, or everything, from a set of size #{n}.}
  \li{#{\displaystyle \binom{n}{1} = n}. Again, we can see this from the formula, or by reasoning that if we want to pick exactly one thing out of a set of size #{n}, we have #{n} ways to do it, one for each element.}
  \li{#{\displaystyle \binom{n}{k} = \binom{n}{n-k}} (each row of Pascal's triangle is a palindrome). In the formula, #{k!} and #{(n-k)!} just switch places; combinatorially, we have the same number of ways to choose #{k} things as we do to choose which #{n-k} things we are \em{not} going to choose. In other words, instead of thinking in terms of “choosing #{k} things” we can think of it in terms of “splitting a set of size #{n} into two subsets of sizes #{k} and #{n-k}”.}
  \li{#{\displaystyle \binom{n}{k} = \binom{n-1}{k} + \binom{n-1}{k-1}} (each entry in Pascal's triangle is the sum of the entry above it and the entry above and to the left). This is the rule for constructing Pascal’s Triangle: each entry is the sum of the entry above it and the one above and to the left. I leave it as a challenge for you to prove this either from the formula or by thinking about it combinatorially.}
  \li{#{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n}{k} = 2^n} (the sum of the #{n}th row of Pascal's triangle is #{2^n}). These are just two different ways to count all the subsets of a set of size #{n}.}
}
}
